The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
clocks = <&mb_clk24mhz>, <&soc_smc50mhz>;
```
```c
clocks = <&v2m_sysctl 0>, <&v2m_sysctl 1>, <&mb_clk24mhz>;
```
```c
clocks = <&v2m_sysctl 2>, <&v2m_sysctl 3>, <&mb_clk24mhz>;
```
Reason:
The absence of the SP810 System Controller in the device tree meant that the SP804 timers could not select the correct clock sources (1MHz or 32kHz), leading to incorrect clock frequencies being used, which caused potential timing issues in the system.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
v2m_refclk1mhz: refclk1mhz {
    clock-frequency = <1000000>;
};
```
```c
v2m_refclk32khz: refclk32khz {
    clock-frequency = <32768>;
};
```
```c
v2m_sysctl: sysctl@020000 {
    compatible = "arm,sp810", "arm,primecell";
    clocks = <&v2m_refclk32khz>, <&v2m_refclk1mhz>;
};
```
Reason:
The addition of the SP810 System Controller and the fixed clock definitions for both 1MHz and 32kHz ensures that the SP804 timers can now select the appropriate clock sources, thus fixing the clock frequency issues and ensuring proper timing functionality in the system.

